WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.880
I'm David Brockway. I'm the manager
of the Great Men Project.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.120
We deliver workshops
on gender equality

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.120
to teenage boys in schools
across London and the South East.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.280
Our topics include sexual harassment,
homophobia, pornography and consent.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.160
But especially, we talk about
masculinity, feminism

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.040
and what it really means to be a man.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.080
Adult men don't really talk about
these issues very much.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.840
We want to get
that conversation normalised

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.080
starting at an early age.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.920
We use participatory activities,
such as word association games.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.440
These allow the boys
to explore stereotypes

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:39.520
that might be in their heads.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.520
We'll ask them to talk about the
first word that comes into their head

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.000
when they think of a certain topic.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.280
For instance, when they're
asked to think about men

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.560
they may come up
with brave, strong or daring.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.400
For women, they may come up
with gentle, kind and caring.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.560
So then we ask how and why?

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.480
What causes these stereotypes?

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.480
Where do the boys get ideas like

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.120
it's weak for men to cry,
or men are strong, women are weak.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.800
Most of the boys have never
had conversations like this before.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.440
Our most powerful moments
are when we have lightbulb moments.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.400
When boys have the opportunity,
through talking to each other,

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.720
to realise that some of these ideas
that they think they know

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.640
are actually a result of conditioning
and the world around them.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.200
Toxic masculinity is talked
about a lot these days.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.280
We would define it as..

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.920
On top of the litany
of negative consequences

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.120
that these behaviours
have for women and girls,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.160
there are also many negative
consequences for men and boys.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.640
At the top of this list would be
mental ill health and suicide.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.520
Suicide rates in this country

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.200
are currently much higher
for men than they are for women.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.320
In our workshops we find
that boys have a lot of difficulty

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.800
expressing their emotions,
accepting emotional vulnerability

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.760
and admitting to any
form of weakness.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.800
Our workshops encourage boys
to critique phrases such as,

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.800
"Man up" and "Take it like a man."

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.120
Which are really saying
boys don’t cry,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.320
and boys don’t ask for help
when they're feeling down.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.560
The phrase "Man up"
is particularly important for us.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.640
It's two words that, put together,
mean absolutely nothing,

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.680
but any man can tell you that,
when told to do so,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.320
they know exactly what
was expected of them at that time.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.280
For years, the responsibility
of dealing with these problems

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.200
has been on women.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.920
Grappling with sexism, sexual
harassment and harassment at work.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.360
We feel that solving these issues
is equally the responsibility of men.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.240
That's why it's so important to get
these conversations started early,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.280
to bring boys into this discussion
so they can have a chance

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.280
to not take forward those
negative ideas and stereotypes.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.200
One of our most difficult
topics is pornography.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.240
Our approach is not to shame anyone,
but to ask questions such as,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.600
"is this a good place
to learn about sex?",

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.600
"Can this be degrading?" and
"Where's the consent in this?"

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.600
We aim to educate the boys
about the porn industry

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.960
to tell them that it can often be
unsafe and coercive for women,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.200
that the things on display
are rarely representative

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.880
of a mutually pleasurable
sexual experience.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.720
Sometimes the boys we work with

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.040
go back to a home environment

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.560
that is contrary
to all of the things we've discussed.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.560
What they are taught
and learn from the media

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.840
is the total opposite
of discussions they had with us.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.280
Healthy relationships are about
respecting, caring and communicating.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.360
Our aim is to plant a seed

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.880
that will allow the boys we work with
to start questioning these things

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.160
and create their own version
of being a man.

